## Proof

**Claim:** Yes, it is true. If $f:\mathbb{Z}^n\to\mathbb{C}$ (or $\mathbb{R}$) satisfies $\|f\|_1=\sum_{k\in\mathbb{Z}^n}|f(k)|=\infty$, then
$$\sup\bigl\{|f(a)|:a\in C_0(\mathbb{Z}^n),\;\|a\|_\infty\le 1\bigr\}=\infty,$$
where $f(a):=\sum_{k\in\mathbb{Z}^n}f(k)\,a(k)$.

---

**Proof.**

Assume $\|f\|_1=\sum_{k\in\mathbb{Z}^n}|f(k)|=\infty$.

Let $M>0$ be arbitrary. Since the series $\sum_k |f(k)|$ diverges, there exists a **finite** set $F\subset\mathbb{Z}^n$ such that
$$\sum_{k\in F}|f(k)|>M.$$

Define a sequence $a:\mathbb{Z}^n\to\mathbb{C}$ by
$$a(k)=\begin{cases}\dfrac{\overline{f(k)}}{|f(k)|}&\text{if }k\in F\text{ and }f(k)\neq 0,\\[6pt]0&\text{otherwise.}\end{cases}$$
(Over $\mathbb{R}$, take $a(k)=\operatorname{sgn}(f(k))$ for $k\in F$.)

**$a\in C_0(\mathbb{Z}^n)$:** The support of $a$ is contained in the finite set $F$, so $a$ vanishes outside a finite set; in particular $a(k)\to 0$ as $\|k\|\to\infty$.

**$\|a\|_\infty\le 1$:** For every $k$, either $a(k)=0$ or $|a(k)|=\bigl|\overline{f(k)}/|f(k)|\bigr|=1$.

**Evaluation:** Since $a$ is finitely supported, the sum $f(a)=\sum_k f(k)\,a(k)$ is a finite sum:
$$f(a)=\sum_{k\in F}f(k)\cdot\frac{\overline{f(k)}}{|f(k)|}=\sum_{k\in F}\frac{|f(k)|^2}{|f(k)|}=\sum_{k\in F}|f(k)|>M.$$

Hence $|f(a)|>M$.

Since $M>0$ was arbitrary, we conclude
$$\sup\bigl\{|f(a)|:a\in C_0(\mathbb{Z}^n),\;\|a\|_\infty\le 1\bigr\}=\infty.\qquad\blacksquare$$

---

**Remark.** This is the converse direction of the classical duality $(C_0(\mathbb{Z}^n))^*\cong l^1(\mathbb{Z}^n)$. A sequence $f$ defines a bounded linear functional on $C_0(\mathbb{Z}^n)$ via the pairing $a\mapsto\sum_k f(k)a(k)$ **if and only if** $f\in l^1(\mathbb{Z}^n)$, and in that case the operator norm equals $\|f\|_1$. The proof above shows that when $\|f\|_1=\infty$, the functional is unbounded on the closed unit ball of $C_0(\mathbb{Z}^n)$ — finitely supported "sign" sequences serve as test functions that realize arbitrarily large values of $|f(a)|$.

